**What was removed, in every case:** `"Domaine de définition"est modifié:` — nothing else. In the glossary that's a *section header* announcing the DOMAINE DE DEFINITION section was amended at edition 2026/2. The parser read DESCRIPTION up to the next ALL-CAPS keyword and swallowed the marker line. Same parser, same marker family, same five zones as BE-X10-015 — which stripped `"Présence"est modifiée:` and `"Code anomalie..."est modifié:` out of Field Length and Format.

## Two judgement calls worth your attention

**I kept the `Jusqu'à 2026/1 inclus:` / `À partir de 2026/2:` split.** It reads like an edition note, but it's the NSSO's own published DESCRIPTION text, not an extraction artefact — and it's load-bearing: on 00071 the *computation itself* changes between editions (last activity salary transposed to full-time → monthly reference × months × coefficient). Deleting it would have destroyed genuine explanation.

**Nothing was relocated because nothing needed to be.** The marker's referent — the definition-domain body — is already carried in full in column O (Option Values), untouched. The one fact the marker itself carried (that these five domains were amended at edition 2026/2, published 01/06/2026) went into a new REMARKS row on each workbook's Version Control sheet.

**Zone 00071 detail:** it ran to 718 chars with the marker, so the builder's 700-char cap fired *inside* the marker, leaving `... " Domaine de...`. That's why a naive grep for `est modifi` finds only 4 of 5 zones — the fifth hides behind its own truncation. Removing the marker leaves 682 chars and the ellipsis goes with it; the genuine text was complete at 682 all along.

